the target language is: lps.

% Concurrent actions, and a precondition over a set of them.
% Source: legacy_lps1/examples/CLOUT_workshop/diningPhilosophers.pl

the maximum time is 7.

the events are:
    *a philosopher* dines; known as dine.

the actions are:
    *a philosopher* picks up *a fork*; known as pickup.
    *a philosopher* puts down *a fork*; known as putdown.

the fluents are:
    *a fork* is available; known as available.

the templates are:
    *a person* is a philosopher.
    *a first fork* and *a second fork* are the forks of *a philosopher*; known as adjacent.

the knowledge base dining philosophers includes:

initially fork1 is available
    and fork2 is available
    and fork3 is available
    and fork4 is available
    and fork5 is available.

socrates is a philosopher.
plato is a philosopher.
aristotle is a philosopher.
hume is a philosopher.
kant is a philosopher.

fork1 and fork2 are the forks of socrates.
fork2 and fork3 are the forks of plato.
fork3 and fork4 are the forks of aristotle.
fork4 and fork5 are the forks of hume.
fork5 and fork1 are the forks of kant.

if a person is a philosopher
then the person dines from a first time to a second time.

a philosopher dines from a first time to a third time if
    a first fork and a second fork are the forks of the philosopher
    and the philosopher picks up the first fork from the first time to a second time
    and the philosopher picks up the second fork from the first time to the second time
    and the philosopher puts down the first fork from the second time to the third time
    and the philosopher puts down the second fork from the second time to the third time.

when a philosopher picks up a fork
then it is not the case that the fork is available.

when a philosopher puts down a fork
then the fork is available.

it must not be true that
    a philosopher picks up a fork from a first time to a second time
    and it is not the case that the fork is available at the first time.

it must not be true that
    a philosopher picks up a fork from a first time to a second time
    and a second philosopher picks up the fork from the first time to the second time
    and the philosopher is different from the second philosopher.
